From 4d27dc852749a6b59e74f6888f4ad17ee6bb08ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 18:10:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 6: never free a tagged immediate whose wrapped
 count reads 1

Nullary variants of taggable shared enums are immediates pointing at a
per-tag static dummy box. Under the TBI encoding rc.inc stores to the
dummy's count unconditionally (the comment assumed a 64-bit count; the
count word is 32 bits), and rc.set, the only decrementing store, skips it.
So the count only grows: after 2^32 references to one nullary constructor
it wrapped to 1, the next decrement took the unique branch and freed the
static dummy (SIGSEGV in mi_free).

Leave rc.inc unguarded (a guard on its store costs up to 30% on rc-heavy
code on some cores and hides the count from LLVM on fresh cells), and make
the decrement's unique branch test the value against the type's nullary
immediates (rc.compare_immortal, only emitted for taggable types under the
special-pointer-tag scheme; skipped for destructuring decrements of an arm
with members). For an immediate the branch releases nothing and yields no
token, so a wrapped count can never free or reuse the dummy. Since the
dummy's count now passes through 0, rc.inc under TBI only assumes
old >= 1 when the pointer's top byte is zero.
